@anon.9bf146a73cb71d9c7e3fe69f46c42f4a.850 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [5158 x i8] }> <{ [5158 x i8] c"Counts through pages, elements, and more.\0A\0AWith the counter function, you can access and modify counters for pages,\0Aheadings, figures, and more. Moreover, you can define custom counters for\0Aother things you want to count.\0A\0ASince counters change throughout the course of the document, their current\0Avalue is _contextual._ It is recommended to read the chapter on [context]\0Abefore continuing here.\0A\0A# Accessing a counter { #accessing }\0ATo access the raw value of a counter, we can use the [`get`]($counter.get)\0Afunction. This function returns an [array]: Counters can have multiple\0Alevels (in the case of headings for sections, subsections, and so on), and\0Aeach item in the array corresponds to one level.\0A\0A```example\0A#set heading(numbering: \221.\22)\0A\0A= Introduction\0ARaw value of heading counter is\0A#context counter(heading).get()\0A```\0A\0A# Displaying a counter { #displaying }\0AOften, we want to display the value of a counter in a more human-readable\0Away. To do that, we can call the [`display`]($counter.display) function on\0Athe counter. This function retrieves the current counter value and formats\0Ait either with a provided or with an automatically inferred [numbering].\0A\0A```example\0A#set heading(numbering: \221.\22)\0A\0A= Introduction\0ASome text here.\0A\0A= Background\0AThe current value is: #context {\0A  counter(heading).display()\0A}\0A\0AOr in roman numerals: #context {\0A  counter(heading).display(\22I\22)\0A}\0A```\0A\0A# Modifying a counter { #modifying }\0ATo modify a counter, you can use the `step` and `update` methods:\0A\0A- The `step` method increases the value of the counter by one. Because\0A  counters can have multiple levels , it optionally takes a `level`\0A  argument. If given, the counter steps at the given depth.\0A\0A- The `update` method allows you to arbitrarily modify the counter. In its\0A  basic form, you give it an integer (or an array for multiple levels). For\0A  more flexibility, you can instead also give it a function that receives\0A  the current value and returns a new value.\0A\0AThe heading counter is stepped before the heading is displayed, so\0A`Analysis` gets the number seven even though the counter is at six after the\0Asecond update.\0A\0A```example\0A#set heading(numbering: \221.\22)\0A\0A= Introduction\0A#counter(heading).step()\0A\0A= Background\0A#counter(heading).update(3)\0A#counter(heading).update(n => n * 2)\0A\0A= Analysis\0ALet's skip 7.1.\0A#counter(heading).step(level: 2)\0A\0A== Analysis\0AStill at #context {\0A  counter(heading).display()\0A}\0A```\0A\0A# Page counter\0AThe page counter is special. It is automatically stepped at each pagebreak.\0ABut like other counters, you can also step it manually. For example, you\0Acould have Roman page numbers for your preface, then switch to Arabic page\0Anumbers for your main content and reset the page counter to one.\0A\0A```example\0A>>> #set page(\0A>>>   height: 100pt,\0A>>>   margin: (bottom: 24pt, rest: 16pt),\0A>>> )\0A#set page(numbering: \22(i)\22)\0A\0A= Preface\0AThe preface is numbered with\0Aroman numerals.\0A\0A#set page(numbering: \221 / 1\22)\0A#counter(page).update(1)\0A\0A= Main text\0AHere, the counter is reset to one.\0AWe also display both the current\0Apage and total number of pages in\0AArabic numbers.\0A```\0A\0A# Custom counters\0ATo define your own counter, call the `counter` function with a string as a\0Akey. This key identifies the counter globally.\0A\0A```example\0A#let mine = counter(\22mycounter\22)\0A#context mine.display() \\\0A#mine.step()\0A#context mine.display() \\\0A#mine.update(c => c * 3)\0A#context mine.display()\0A```\0A\0A# How to step\0AWhen you define and use a custom counter, in general, you should first step\0Athe counter and then display it. This way, the stepping behaviour of a\0Acounter can depend on the element it is stepped for. If you were writing a\0Acounter for, let's say, theorems, your theorem's definition would thus first\0Ainclude the counter step and only then display the counter and the theorem's\0Acontents.\0A\0A```example\0A#let c = counter(\22theorem\22)\0A#let theorem(it) = block[\0A  #c.step()\0A  *Theorem #context c.display():*\0A  #it\0A]\0A\0A#theorem[$1 = 1$]\0A#theorem[$2 < 3$]\0A```\0A\0AThe rationale behind this is best explained on the example of the heading\0Acounter: An update to the heading counter depends on the heading's level. By\0Astepping directly before the heading, we can correctly step from `1` to\0A`1.1` when encountering a level 2 heading. If we were to step after the\0Aheading, we wouldn't know what to step to.\0A\0ABecause counters should always be stepped before the elements they count,\0Athey always start at zero. This way, they are at one for the first display\0A(which happens after the first step).\0A\0A# Time travel\0ACounters can travel through time! You can find out the final value of the\0Acounter before it is reached and even determine what the value was at any\0Aparticular location in the document.\0A\0A```example\0A#let mine = counter(\22mycounter\22)\0A\0A= Values\0A#context [\0A  Value here: #mine.get() \\\0A  At intro: #mine.at(<intro>) \\\0A  Final value: #mine.final()\0A]\0A\0A#mine.update(n => n + 3)\0A\0A= Introduction <intro>\0A#lorem(10)\0A\0A#mine.step()\0A#mine.step()\0A```\0A\0A# Other kinds of state { #other-state }\0AThe `counter` type is closely related to [state] type. Read its\0Adocumentation for more details on state management in Typst and why it\0Adoesn't just use normal variables for counters." }>, align 1
